<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elbląg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elbing, Elblag</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elbląg is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>. Founded as Elbing in 1237 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, it received Lübeck law and became a leading Hanseatic port. It rebelled against the Order and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> as part of Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1466, then to Prussia in 1772. Heavily damaged in 1945, it returned to Poland after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elblag.jpg" alt="Elbląg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sławomir Milejski · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SM_Elbl%C4%85g_Ko%C5%9Bci%C3%B3%C5%82_%C5%9Bw_Miko%C5%82aja_(3)_ID_644686.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Elbląg lies in the Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the eastern edge of the Żuławy region. It stands on the river Elbląg, which connects Lake Drużno with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a> about 10 km to the north, and it is about 55 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. Its name comes from the German Elbing, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> gave both to the river and to the citadel they established on its bank in 1237 to prevent the Old Prussian settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truso">Truso</a> from being reoccupied. The oldest known mention of the river or town is in the form Ylfing, in the report of the sailor Wulfstan from the end of the 9th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicon-terrae-prussiae">Chronicon terrae Prussiae</a> describes the founding of Elbing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-balk">Hermann Balk</a>, after the knights built two ships, the Pilgerim and the Vridelant, and used them to clear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a> of Prussians. In February 1440 the city hosted a convention that founded the anti-Teutonic Prussian Confederation. In 1454 the Confederation revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> and petitioned King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a>, who signed the act of incorporation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in March 1454, which began the Thirteen Years' War. Elbing received privileges like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, including the right to mint Polish coins. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> of 1466 the Order renounced its claims and recognised the city as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p>Elbląg was a major port for English–Polish trade until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it and its trading role weakened. The Elbląg Canal was commissioned during 19th-century industrialisation. French troops occupied the city without resistance in January 1807, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> held a parade there on 8 May. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Germans ran a prison, forced labour subcamps and three subcamps of the Stutthof concentration camp in the city. The Old Town was almost completely destroyed at the end of the war, in fighting that began on 23 January 1945, and the city passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. About 98 per cent of its new inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> expelled from the former eastern Polish areas annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Elbląg has more than 120,000 inhabitants and is the capital of Elbląg County. Parts of the inner city were rebuilt gradually, and from around 2000 rebuilding began in a style imitating the earlier architecture, often over the old foundations and with old bricks. Surviving buildings include St Nicholas Cathedral, a 13th-century Gothic church that became a cathedral only in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elbląg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1440 they founded the Prussian Confederation at Marienwerder (Kwidzyn), led by the citizens of Danzig, <b>Elbing</b> and Thorn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1230 the Landmeister Hermann Balk led the knights across the Vistula, founding Thorn in 1231, Kulm in 1232 and <b>Elbing</b> in 1237.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pogesania lay along the Vistula Lagoon east of the <b>Elbląg</b> river, in modern northern Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/pogesanians">Pogesanians</a></li></ul></section>
